# i18n rules

- No hardcoded user-facing strings. Every visible text, `aria-label`, `title`, `alt`, and `placeholder`
  goes through `t()` from react-i18next. `src/i18n/noHardcodedStrings.test.ts` enforces this for `src/ui`.
- Turkish (`tr`) is the default and the reference locale: add new keys to `src/i18n/locales/tr/*` first,
  then to every other locale. `src/i18n/i18n.test.ts` fails on missing/extra keys, missing plural forms,
  or mismatched `{{placeholders}}`.
- Namespaces: `common` (UI), `bots` (titles, taglines, speech lines per bot id — not names), `coach` (post-game templates),
  `puzzles` (puzzle/revenge screens, theme names), `about` (legal/About page).
  Bot lines and coach templates are per language, never assembled from English fragments.
- Core code returns keys + raw params (piece symbols, SAN, squares). The UI localizes them:
  piece names via `common:pieces.*`, notation via `useLocalizedSan()` (`common:notation`).
  Coach strings receive `context` = piece symbol so translators may add gendered variants (`hangingPiece_q`).
- Use i18next plurals (`key_one`, `key_other`, plus `_few`/`_many` where the language needs them).
- Adding a language: add it to `LOCALES` in `src/i18n/locales.ts` (mark `status: 'beta'` if machine-translated)
  and create `src/i18n/locales/<code>/{common,bots,coach,puzzles,about}.json`. Nothing else should need changing.
- Layout: CSS logical properties only (`margin-inline`, `padding-block`, `inset-inline-start`),
  no fixed widths on text, allow wrapping. The board stays `direction: ltr` even in RTL locales.
- Locale files are lazy-loaded per language; never import a locale JSON statically from app code.
- Bot proper names stay Turkish in every locale; render them with `lang="tr"`.
- Beta locales show a "report translation error" mail link (locale + screen in the subject); keep it working when adding screens.
